          "theory_html": "<section id=\"m1-s1-ready\"><h3>Getting ready for practical work</h3><p>Kitchen readiness means preparing yourself to work safely before practical food work begins. It is not just about being organised; it is about getting into a safe way of thinking and working. In Food Technology, readiness, hygiene and safety are taught before practical work because they help you notice what is happening around you and make sensible choices. A student who is ready listens carefully, pays attention to the work area and follows the teacher’s directions and local procedures.</p><p>Before practical work, think about what you are about to do and what could make the situation less safe. A hazard is something that could cause harm or create an unsafe situation. You are not expected to guess how every hazard should be managed. Your job is to notice possible problems, follow directions and avoid rushing ahead when you are unsure. Kitchen routines and hygiene reflection also help you think about how your own actions can affect safe, responsible work. Good readiness is shown by being attentive, calm and prepared to follow the routine that your teacher sets.</p></section><section id=\"m1-s1-onguard\"><h3>Why OnGuard comes before practical work</h3><p>OnGuard training is part of your preparation for workshop safety. It is completed before practical work because it helps build the safety knowledge and thinking you need when working in a practical environment. OnGuard is not a replacement for your teacher’s instructions or for the local procedures used in your class. Instead, it supports them by giving you a stronger foundation for recognising safe conduct and understanding why safety needs your full attention.</p><p>The important idea is not simply to finish training and forget it. You should use what you have learned to support your decisions during lessons. When the teacher explains a direction, connect it with the safety ideas you have already studied. If something in a practical lesson is different from what you expected, do not assume that an online example or a remembered routine automatically applies. The teacher’s directions and the procedures for your current lesson control what you do. OnGuard prepares you to think safely; your teacher guides how that safety is applied in your actual food workshop.</p></section><section id=\"m1-s1-safe-decisions\"><h3>Making safe decisions when you are unsure</h3><p>Safe practical work depends on making good decisions, especially when something is unclear. Start by paying attention to the teacher’s directions and to what is happening in the work area. Look for hazards or situations that do not seem right. If you understand the direction and the situation is clear, continue by following the required procedure. If you are unsure, stop and ask before acting. Asking a question is part of safe conduct because it prevents guessing from turning into an unsafe choice.</p><p>This approach also matters when a routine changes or when you cannot remember what to do next. Do not invent a step, copy another group without knowing whether their situation is the same, or rely on what you think usually happens. Instead, pause, identify what is uncertain and ask the teacher for guidance. Safe decisions are based on directions, awareness and careful judgement. The goal is not to work as quickly as possible. The goal is to work in a way that shows you can recognise possible hazards, follow the correct local procedure and seek help when you need it.</p></section>",
