          "theory_html": "<section id=\"m2-s3-six\"><h3>Six nutrient groups, different jobs</h3><p>Nutrients are substances in food that the body uses for different purposes. The six essential nutrient groups are carbohydrates, protein, fats, vitamins, minerals and water. Each group has broad roles, but it is important not to label nutrients as simply good or bad. The body needs different nutrients for different jobs. Carbohydrates and fats can provide energy, protein supports growth and repair, vitamins and minerals support many body processes, and water supports hydration and other body functions. Learning these groups helps you think more carefully about what food can provide.</p><p>Foods do not always fit neatly into one nutrient group. A single food can provide more than one nutrient, so it is misleading to think that a food is only a carbohydrate food, a protein food or a fat food. When you look at a meal or recipe, think about the range of nutrients that may be present rather than trying to give each food only one label. In practical Food Technology, the current teacher recipe tells you what to prepare, while nutrition knowledge helps you understand some of the different roles the foods in a recipe may contribute.</p></section><section id=\"m2-s3-functions\"><h3>Energy, growth, repair and body processes</h3><p>Some nutrient groups are often discussed because of the broad jobs they help with. Carbohydrates and fats can supply energy that the body can use. Protein supports growth and repair. Vitamins and minerals support body processes, which means they help the body carry out many of its normal functions. Water supports hydration and also helps with body functions. These descriptions are broad on purpose. A nutrient can have more than one role, and the body works through many connected processes rather than using each nutrient for only one simple job.</p><p>A useful way to remember these roles is to connect nutrients with body needs rather than with good or bad labels. For example, energy is a body need, but that does not mean one nutrient should be judged as better than every other nutrient. Growth, repair, hydration and normal body processes are also important needs. The six nutrient groups contribute in different ways, so understanding their roles is more useful than trying to rank them. When discussing a food or recipe, use careful language such as &#39;can provide&#39; or &#39;supports&#39; rather than claiming that one food alone meets every need.</p></section><section id=\"m2-s3-variety\"><h3>Why variety matters</h3><p>Because different nutrient groups support different body needs, eating a variety of foods can help provide a broader range of nutrients. Variety means including different types of foods rather than relying on the same foods all the time. It also reminds us that foods can contribute several nutrients at once. Looking at variety is more useful than dividing foods into perfect and poor choices, because nutrition is about the combination of foods and nutrients across what a person eats.</p><p>When studying a recipe, you can practise identifying several possible nutrient groups without assuming that each ingredient supplies only one. You can also think about whether different foods contribute to different needs such as energy, growth, repair, hydration and body processes. In class practicals, always follow the current teacher recipe, teacher directions and local procedures. Your nutrition knowledge is there to help you explain what nutrients do and why variety matters, not to change the recipe or make medical judgements about what another person should eat.</p></section>",
