          "theory_html": "<section id=\"m3-s1-outcome\"><h3>Plan from the finished result backwards</h3><p>Workflow is an organised plan for moving from preparation to a finished food product and a clean work area. Before beginning, read the complete current recipe so you understand what the practical is expected to achieve. Look at the method as a whole rather than focusing only on the first instruction. Thinking about the finished result can help you work backwards and identify what needs to happen before later parts of the practical can begin. This makes the practical easier to manage because you have a clearer picture of where the work is heading.</p><p>Some tasks depend on other tasks being completed first. This is called a dependency. For example, if one part of a recipe must be ready before another part can continue, that relationship affects the workflow. You do not need to invent these dependencies yourself; identify them by carefully reading the current teacher recipe. This applies to practicals such as Savoury Twists and Savoury Bread Cases. The Bread Cases recipe includes bread cases, a roux and listed filling options, but the current recipe and teacher directions control the exact order and practical details.</p></section><section id=\"m3-s1-sequence\"><h3>Put tasks in a workable order</h3><p>A good workflow puts tasks into a logical sequence. Start by identifying what needs to be prepared first, what depends on something else, and what can be completed later. Following the recipe sequence helps prevent confusion and reduces the chance of reaching a step before you are ready for it. Good planning does not mean rushing. It means knowing what comes next and organising your actions so that the practical moves steadily from one stage to another.</p><p>Sometimes a practical includes periods when you are waiting before the next recipe action can happen. Waiting time can be used purposefully. Instead of standing still or jumping ahead without checking, look at the current recipe and teacher directions to see what appropriate task can be completed. This may include checking your progress, organising your work area or completing clean-up that fits the stage you have reached. Do not assume that every spare moment means you should start another recipe step. If you are unsure whether a task can be done while waiting, ask the teacher before changing the planned sequence.</p></section><section id=\"m3-s1-monitor\"><h3>Check progress and adjust safely</h3><p>Workflow planning continues while you work. Monitor your progress by checking where you are in the current recipe and what still needs to be completed. Ask yourself whether earlier tasks are finished, whether the next step depends on something else, and whether your work area is staying organised. These checks help you recognise problems before they create more confusion. They also help you manage the whole practical, including the final clean-up stage, rather than focusing only on producing the food.</p><p>A plan may need attention if something is unclear or progress is different from what you expected. This does not mean you should invent a new method or change the recipe by yourself. Stop, check the current recipe and ask the teacher before altering an unclear step or sequence. Good workflow is flexible in a controlled way: you monitor what is happening, use the information you have and seek guidance when needed. A practical is properly managed when the recipe is followed, progress is checked, tasks are completed in a sensible order and the work area is left clean according to local procedures.</p></section>",
