          "theory_html": "<section id=\"m4-s2-purpose\"><h3>Turn a food idea into a clear order</h3><p>A food order is planning information that communicates what is needed for a practical food activity. It turns an approved recipe or food plan into a clear record of the items and quantities required. The current teacher-approved recipe or plan is the source of this information. Students should not rely on memory, guess what might be needed or add items that are not part of the approved plan. A clear order helps the practical move from an idea on paper towards organised production.</p><p>Accuracy matters because the order needs to represent the practical that is actually planned. Start with the current approved recipe or plan and work through it carefully. Identify each required item and record the quantity exactly as it is provided. If information is unclear, stop and ask rather than inventing a quantity or making a substitution. The exact way a food order is recorded or processed can vary between schools, so teacher directions and local procedures always control how the order is completed and used.</p></section><section id=\"m4-s2-check\"><h3>Check names, amounts and planned use</h3><p>A first draft of a food order should always be checked. Compare each entry with the current approved recipe or plan. Check that item names match the source information and that quantities have been copied accurately. Then look for missing entries and accidental duplicates. A missing item may leave the practical without something it requires, while a duplicated entry may request more than the plan needs. Checking before the order is finalised is easier than discovering an avoidable mistake during practical work.</p><p>It is also useful to connect each ordered item with its planned use. You do not need to invent extra recipe steps; simply make sure that every entry can be traced back to the approved recipe or plan. If an item appears on the order but you cannot explain where it came from, check the source again. If something in the source is unclear, ask the teacher. This check supports responsible resource use because the order is based on what the practical actually requires rather than guesses, unnecessary duplication or remembered information from another activity.</p></section><section id=\"m4-s2-manage\"><h3>Connect the order to the workflow</h3><p>A food order is connected to workflow because planning what is needed supports the way a practical is organised. Workflow is the planned movement from preparation through production and clean-up. If the order accurately reflects the current recipe or plan, students can approach the practical with a clearer understanding of the resources required. The order does not replace the recipe or method. Instead, it supports them by showing that the required items have been considered before production begins.</p><p>Good organisation continues once practical work starts. Students still need to read the current recipe, follow the planned sequence, monitor progress and use resources responsibly. If the practical information changes under teacher direction, do not automatically assume an earlier order or remembered list still applies. Check the current approved information. Food ordering, workflow and practical management are connected parts of the same design process: decide what is being made, identify what is needed, check the order for accuracy, then use the approved plan to guide production. Teacher directions and local procedures control the exact ordering and practical process.</p></section>",
